# errors-runtime.topic -- run-time failures, native-backend failures, file
# format errors (.hir, .hirx, .ir), compiler limits, and SURFACE SYNTAX.

@topic error.arithmetic
title: ARITHMETIC: an arithmetic operation with no defined result
summary: CORE SEMANTIC ARITHMETIC stops a run when an arithmetic operation has no defined result; today the only such operation is mod with a divisor of zero.
index: errors
alias: mod by zero; division by zero; modulo by zero
keywords: mod, zero, divisor, remainder, euclidean, run time
related: stdlib.mod capability.integer-division concept.integers error.range
docs: README.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC ARITHMETIC

== MEANING
Run time, on every backend; an error of the program. mod(a, b) (written
a.mod(b)) is Euclidean: its result r satisfies 0 <= r < |b| whatever the
signs. With b = 0 no such r exists, and the run stops with
"mod: division by zero".

It is not a declared error: no `on` handler catches it and no `errors`
clause admits it. The compiler does not prove divisors nonzero, and it does
not reject a literal zero either: mod(7, 0) compiles and fails when it
runs.

== EXAMPLE
    fn bucket(hash: int, size: int) -> int:
        hash.mod(size)

    bucket(17, 0)

compiles, and stops on every backend with

    error: mod: division by zero (CORE SEMANTIC ARITHMETIC)

== WHAT TO DO
Decide what the program means when the divisor is zero, and test for it
before the call:

    fn bucket(hash: int, size: int) -> int:
        if size == 0:
            return 0
        hash.mod(size)

If zero is a caller's mistake rather than a case, fail with a declared
error of your own instead, so callers must handle it (concept.errors).
Botlish has no integer division (capability.integer-division); Float64
division by zero is the declared error NonFiniteFloat (concept.floats).

@topic error.range
title: RANGE: an argument outside the range an operation accepts
summary: CORE SEMANTIC RANGE stops a run when an intrinsic receives an argument outside the range it accepts: a shift amount, a capacity or length, a one-character string, a field too wide for its encoding.
index: errors
alias: shift amount out of range; capacity out of range; argument out of range
keywords: shift_left, shift_right, capacity, mutable_array, is_tcl_alpha, binary::emit, length, ceiling, run time
related: stdlib.shift_left stdlib.shift_right stdlib.mutable_array::create proof.list-bounds error.arithmetic concept.integers
docs: README.md MUTABLE-ARRAY.md BINARY-LAYOUT.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC RANGE

== MEANING
Run time, on every backend; normally an error of the program. Some
intrinsics accept only part of their parameter type and check the rest as
they run:
  * shift_left, shift_right: the shift amount must be in 0..1048576;
  * mutable_array::create, allocate and generate: the capacity must be in
    0..4611686018427387903 (2^62 - 1);
  * str::is_tcl_alpha, str::is_tcl_alnum: the string must hold exactly
    one character;
  * mutable_byte_store::zeroed, replace, freeze_prefix: lengths, counts
    and indices within the store;
  * binary::emit: each field's value must fit its encoding;
  * the native runtime's ceiling: a String, List or byte store longer
    than 2^62 - 1, or a byte store it cannot allocate (out of memory).

RANGE is not a declared error: no handler catches it. It is not what an
index past the end produces: list::at, mutable_array::at and str::char_at
fail with the declared error IndexNotFound, and str::substring with
LowerUnderrun or UpperOverrun, which a program handles or the compiler
proves impossible (proof.list-bounds).

== EXAMPLE
    fn shifted(x: int, k: int) -> int:
        x.shift_left(k)

    shifted(1, -1)

compiles, and stops on every backend with

    error: shift amount out of range (0..1048576) (CORE SEMANTIC RANGE)

A negative capacity:

    error: mutable_array::create: capacity must be 0..4611686018427387903,
    got -1 (CORE SEMANTIC RANGE)

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message names the operation and the range it accepts. Find where the
offending argument was computed: it is usually a difference or a count that
can be negative, or a String where one character was expected.

== WHAT TO DO
Test the argument before the call (`if k < 0 or k > 64: ...`) or give the
parameter an integer domain that excludes the bad values
(concept.integer-domains), so callers must establish the range.

@topic error.equality
title: EQUALITY: == or hash on a value that has no equality
summary: CORE SEMANTIC EQUALITY stops a run when == (or a hash) reaches a function, a native, a MutableArray, a MutableVector, a coroutine handle or a C pointer, which have no defined equality.
index: errors
alias: == on functions; function equality; equality of functions; hash of a function
keywords: ==, hash, callable, block, function, mutable array, mutable vector, immutable_set, contains
related: rule.equality stdlib.== stdlib.hash concept.function-types concept.mutable-array concept.mutable-vector error.not-callable
docs: README.md MUTABLE-ARRAY.md M3-EQUALITY-TOTAL-SETCONTAINS-EFFECT.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC EQUALITY

== MEANING
Run time, on every backend; an error of the program. `==` compares values
structurally: Ints by value, strings by characters, Lists and structs
element by element, values of different kinds as unequal. Some values have
no equality at all, because the only equality they could have is identity,
which Botlish does not expose: functions and natives (callables),
MutableArray and MutableVector headers, coroutine handles and C pointers.
`==`, `hash` and the operations built on them (immutable_set::from_list,
immutable_set::contains) stop with EQUALITY when they reach one, also
inside a List or a struct being compared.

The message says "callables" for all of them, MutableArray included:

    == is not defined for callables: A == B
    hash is not defined for callables: V

The front end does not reject these comparisons today, even where the
operands' types show them: they fail when they run. (An affine value's
comparison is the static AFFINE-EQUALITY-UNSUPPORTED instead.)

== EXAMPLE
    fn inc(x):
        x + 1

    fn dec(x):
        x - 1

    inc == dec

compiles, and stops on every backend with

    error: == is not defined for callables: <block (x)> == <block (x)>
    (CORE SEMANTIC EQUALITY)

Two MutableArrays with equal elements fail the same way.

== WHAT TO DO
Compare what the values mean, not the values themselves: for functions, a
name, an enum case or an Int that identifies them; for a MutableArray or
MutableVector, its contents as a List (mutable_array::freeze). Keep
functions out of Lists that are compared or put in a set.

@topic error.native-only
title: NATIVE-ONLY: an operation only the native backends perform
summary: CORE SEMANTIC NATIVE-ONLY stops a run on interp or compile when the program reaches a machine address, a raw Linux system call or a C call, which only the native backends perform.
index: errors backends
alias: native-only error; refused on the Tcl backends; print on interp
keywords: interp, compile, syscall, address, from_bytes, extern c, io, print_line, LinuxIO
related: backend.native-only backend.io task.print-output backend.cranelift concept.c-ffi stdlib.module.io
docs: CONTEXT-TRAITS.md LINUX-X86-64-SYSCALL.md ABI-BYTES.md C-FFI.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC NATIVE-ONLY

== MEANING
Run time, on interp and compile only; not an error of the program, but of
the backend chosen for it. The Tcl backends have no machine memory and do
not imitate the kernel or a C library, so they compile such a program like
any other and refuse the operation when it runs:
  * the address bridges abi::x86_64::from_bytes and from_mutable_bytes
    (and the aarch64 ones);
  * the system-call transports linux::abi::x86_64::syscall and
    linux::abi::aarch64::syscall;
  * a call of an `extern c fn`;
  * reading or writing bytes through a raw C address.
Everything built on them is native-only too: lib/linux.bot, linux::io,
and so io::print_line and the rest of io's printing through
linux::io::LinuxIO.

== EXAMPLE
    import io
    import linux::io

    fn hello() errors io::WriteFailed:
        io::print_line("Hello, world!")

    with context linux::io::create()

    hello():
        on io::WriteFailed:
            unit

prints "Hello, world!" on cranelift; on interp and compile it stops with
(one line, wrapped here)

    error: abi::x86_64::from_bytes produces the machine address of a byte
    buffer, which exists only in the native backend (cranelift, or a
    standalone executable) on Linux x86-64; the Tcl backends (interp,
    compile) have no machine addresses and do not imitate one (CORE
    SEMANTIC NATIVE-ONLY)

== WHAT TO DO
Run it with -backend cranelift (or cranelift-generic), or build an
executable (-emit-native-executable). To keep a program runnable
everywhere, compute its result in portable code and return it as the
program's value, which every backend prints; print only at a native edge
(backend.io).

@topic error.not-boolean
title: NOT-BOOLEAN: an if condition that is not a Boolean
summary: CORE SEMANTIC NOT-BOOLEAN stops a run when an if (or and, or, not) condition evaluates to something other than true or false.
index: errors
alias: condition is not a Boolean; non-Boolean condition
keywords: if, condition, bool, true, false, any, truthy, run time
related: concept.booleans concept.if-expressions concept.types error.not-callable
docs: README.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC NOT-BOOLEAN

== MEANING
Run time, on every backend; an error of the program. A condition must be a
Bool: there is no truthiness, so 0, "", [] and unit are not false. The
message shows the value: "if condition must be a Boolean, got V" -- also
for an operand of `and`, `or` and `not`, which are conditions too.

Where the compiler infers a parameter's type from its use as a condition,
a non-Boolean argument is rejected statically (TYPE). Where it cannot --
a parameter declared `any`, a value from untyped code -- the check happens
when the condition runs. The front end does not reject every condition
whose type it knows today: even `if 3:` compiles and fails here.

== EXAMPLE
    fn choose(flag: any) -> int:
        if flag:
            return 1
        2

    choose(1)

compiles, and stops on every backend with

    error: if condition must be a Boolean, got 1 (CORE SEMANTIC NOT-BOOLEAN)

== WHAT TO DO
Write the comparison you mean (`if n != 0:`, `if s.length() > 0:`) and give
the parameter its type (`flag: bool`), so a wrong argument is a compile-time
TYPE error at the call.

@topic error.not-callable
title: NOT-CALLABLE: calling a value that is not a function
summary: CORE SEMANTIC NOT-CALLABLE stops a run when a call's callee evaluates to something other than a function or a native.
index: errors
alias: call a non-function; non-callable value; calling a number
keywords: call, callee, function value, untyped parameter, any, Fn
related: concept.functions concept.function-types concept.types error.not-boolean error.equality
docs: README.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC NOT-CALLABLE

== MEANING
Run time, on every backend; an error of the program. A call `f(x)` whose
`f` is an Int, a String, a struct or any other non-callable value stops
with "cannot call non-callable value V". The compiler does not establish
that a called parameter or binding holds a function: an untyped parameter
(or one declared `any`) that is called is checked when the call runs.

== EXAMPLE
    fn apply(f, x):
        f(x)

    apply(3, 4)

compiles, and stops on every backend with

    error: cannot call non-callable value 3 (CORE SEMANTIC NOT-CALLABLE)

== WHAT TO DO
Give a parameter that is called a function type, `f: Fn{args: [int],
return: int}` (concept.function-types): a non-function argument is then a
compile-time TYPE error at the call, and the call itself is checked against
the function's contract.

@topic error.struct
title: STRUCT: a struct value built with the wrong fields (core IR)
summary: CORE SEMANTIC STRUCT is raised by the reference interpreter when core IR builds a named struct with fields other than its declaration's; a checked Botlish program never reaches it.
index: errors
alias: struct initializer fields mismatch
keywords: struct, initializer, fields, core IR, ir, interp
related: concept.structs rule.struct-construction error.core-malformed backend.interp
docs: README.md STRUCTS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC STRUCT

== MEANING
Run time, on interp. Core IR's `(struct HEAD NAME EXPR ...)` names a
declaration and its fields; when the written field names differ from the
declaration's, the interpreter stops with "struct ID initializer has
fields {NAMES}, but its declaration has {FIELDS}". In a .bot program the
front end checks every construction statically (MISSING-FIELD,
UNKNOWN-FIELD, DUPLICATE-FIELD, rule.struct-construction), so this code
comes only from core IR text (.ir files) or unchecked HIR.

== EXAMPLE
The core IR file

    {struct {Point x y} x {const 1}}

stops on interp with

    error: struct Point initializer has fields {x}, but its declaration
    has {x y} (CORE SEMANTIC STRUCT)

== WHAT TO DO
Fix the IR text: write every declared field once. From a .bot program it
would be a compiler bug: report the program.

@topic error.uncaught-error
title: UNCAUGHT-ERROR: a declared error reached the top of an unchecked program
summary: CORE SEMANTIC UNCAUGHT-ERROR stops a run when a declared error propagates out of the whole program; a checked program cannot do that, so it comes from core IR text, unchecked HIR or a compiler bug.
index: errors
alias: uncaught propagated error; error escaped the program
keywords: fail, propagate, top level, unhandled, core IR, strict, completion
related: concept.errors rule.unhandled-errors proof.completions error.unhandled-error error.known-error
docs: EXPLICIT-ERROR-COMPLETIONS.md STATIC-COMPLETION-PROOFS.md ARGV.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC UNCAUGHT-ERROR

== MEANING
Run time, on every backend. The program's top level admits no errors, so
in a checked .bot program every declared error that may reach it must be
handled: an unhandled one is the compile-time UNHANDLED-ERROR, a certain
one KNOWN-ERROR. At run time the error is shown as "uncaught propagated
error: <error NAME>". It is reached only by:
  * core IR text (.ir files), which is not checked for errors;
  * a program compiled with its diagnostics suppressed (the API's
    -strict 0), as the cross-backend tests do on purpose;
  * a checked program, only through an unsound completion proof: a
    compiler bug.

== EXAMPLE
The core IR file

    {call {ref list::at} {const list {1 2}} {const 5}}

stops on interp and compile with

    error: uncaught propagated error: <error IndexNotFound>
    (CORE SEMANTIC UNCAUGHT-ERROR)

The same call in a .bot program, [1, 2].at(5), is rejected before it runs
(KNOWN-ERROR).

== WHAT TO DO
In IR text, handle the error with a `(handle ...)` node. From a checked
.bot program, report the program: the compiler accepted a call whose
error it should have required you to handle.

@topic error.unreachable
title: UNREACHABLE: dead code the compiler proved never runs was executed
summary: CORE SEMANTIC UNREACHABLE is a compiler bug: code that no installed context reaches, which static verification proved never runs, was executed on a Tcl backend.
index: errors
alias: context#unreachable
keywords: context trait, dead code, monomorphization, placeholder, compiler bug
related: concept.context-traits concept.traits proof.reachability error.native-bug
docs: CONTEXT-TRAITS.md TRAITS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC UNREACHABLE

== MEANING
Run time; a compiler bug, never an error of the program. Context traits
and traits are resolved before any backend runs (concept.context-traits):
an operation or a call that no installed context reaches -- in statically
unreachable code, or in a nested function that is never called -- is
replaced by a call of the placeholder `context#unreachable()`, because
verification proved it never runs. On interp and compile the placeholder
raises "context#unreachable: code that no installed context reaches was
executed (a compiler bug: static verification proved it never runs)";
native code compiles it to a trap.

== WHAT TO DO
Report the program: the reachability proof was wrong. -contexts and
-traits show what the compiler selected for each call.

@topic error.coroutine-running
title: COROUTINE-RUNNING: a coroutine resumed while it runs
summary: CORE SEMANTIC COROUTINE-RUNNING is a defensive run-time check that a checked program cannot reach: a coroutine was resumed from inside its own running body.
index: errors
alias: coroutine resumes itself
keywords: coroutine, resume, running, affine, handle
related: concept.coroutines concept.affine error.coroutine-state error.yield-outside-coroutine
docs: COROUTINES.md AFFINE-VALUES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC COROUTINE-RUNNING

== MEANING
Run time, on every backend; a compiler bug if a checked program reaches
it. The message is "coroutine N is already running: a coroutine cannot
resume itself" ("the coroutine is already running: ..." natively). A
coroutine handle is an affine value with one owner, and
the body that is running cannot own its own handle (concept.coroutines),
so the runtimes keep this check only as a safety net, as the native
runtime's source says: "Coroutine lifecycle states a checked program
cannot reach (the affine discipline, COROUTINES.md), kept as defensive
runtime checks."

== WHAT TO DO
Report the program, with the backend it failed on.

@topic error.coroutine-state
title: COROUTINE-STATE: a coroutine handle used in an impossible state
summary: CORE SEMANTIC COROUTINE-STATE reports a coroutine started twice, resumed before it started or used after its release (a compiler bug), or a native coroutine stack the system refused.
index: errors
alias: coroutine already started; resume a released coroutine
keywords: coroutine, start, resume, release, done?, handle, state, affine
related: concept.coroutines concept.affine error.coroutine-running stdlib.coroutine::done?
docs: COROUTINES.md AFFINE-VALUES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC COROUTINE-STATE

== MEANING
Run time, on every backend. The messages (natively "the coroutine"
instead of "coroutine N"):

    coroutine N was already started
    coroutine N was never started
    coroutine N was released after its handle's last use: nothing can
      resume it (a compiler bug)
    coroutine handle N does not belong to this program run

are a compiler bug if a checked program reaches them. A coroutine starts
when it is created and is released right after its handle's last use
(concept.coroutines): the compiler places both, so a checked program never
resumes or inspects a coroutine in the wrong state. Resuming a coroutine
that has completed is not this error: it returns the same final result
again.

One form is a resource limit instead, on the native backends only: "cannot
map a N-byte coroutine stack: ...". Each live native coroutine has its own
stack, 8 MiB of address space by default
(BOTLISH_NATIVE_COROUTINE_STACK_BYTES sets it, at least 64 KiB), and the
system refused another one.

== WHAT TO DO
For the stack form, keep fewer coroutines alive at once or give them
smaller stacks. Otherwise report the program, with the backend it failed
on: a release placed too early is the likeliest cause.

@topic error.yield-outside-coroutine
title: YIELD-OUTSIDE-COROUTINE: yield ran with no coroutine running
summary: CORE SEMANTIC YIELD-OUTSIDE-COROUTINE is a defensive run-time check that a checked program cannot reach: a yield executed while no coroutine was running.
index: errors
alias: yield with no coroutine running
keywords: yield, coroutine, effect, run time
related: concept.coroutines error.coroutine-running error.coroutine-state
docs: COROUTINES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC YIELD-OUTSIDE-COROUTINE

== MEANING
Run time; a compiler bug if a checked program reaches it. The message is
"yield outside a coroutine: no coroutine is running here". `yield` is a
statically tracked effect: the compiler rejects a yield that is not inside
a function a coroutine runs (YIELD-OUTSIDE-FUNCTION, UNWRAPPED-YIELD and
the other coroutine diagnostics), so only core IR text or unchecked HIR
can execute one outside a coroutine.

== WHAT TO DO
Report the program if it was a checked .bot program.

@topic error.core-contract-type
title: CORE CONTRACT TYPE: a native broke its declared type contract
summary: CORE CONTRACT TYPE means trusted Tcl code -- a native's implementation -- returned or accepted a value outside its declared types; it is a bug in the runtime, never in the program.
index: errors
alias: trusted native broke its contract; contract violation of a native
keywords: native, contract, registry, result type, implementation bug, Tcl runtime
related: stdlib.module.root backend.interp backend.compile error.native-bug
docs: README.md
code: CORE CONTRACT TYPE

== MEANING
Run time, on interp and compile; a bug in the Tcl runtime, not in the
program. Every native is registered with parameter and result types,
which the type checker trusts; so after each native call the runtime
checks that the implementation kept its promise. A violation reads

    NAME result: contract violation: expected T, got V
    NAME argument I: contract violation: expected T, got V

or says that the native signalled an error it does not declare, or ended
with a completion it may not. A native added by a test or an embedding
tool (core::native::register) can raise it about its own implementation.

== WHAT TO DO
Report it with the program: the native named in the message is wrong. Run
the program on another backend meanwhile.

@topic error.core-declared-error
title: CORE DECLARED-ERROR: a native's declared error escaped its call
summary: CORE DECLARED-ERROR NAME is the runtime's internal signal for a native failing with a declared error; seen by a user, it escaped the call that converts it, which is a compiler or runtime bug.
index: errors
alias: failDeclared escaped
keywords: native, declared error, IndexNotFound, compile backend, direct call, bounds proof
related: proof.list-bounds proof.completions backend.compile error.uncaught-error
docs: EXPLICIT-ERROR-COMPLETIONS.md STATIC-COMPLETION-PROOFS.md
code: CORE DECLARED-ERROR

== MEANING
A Tcl native fails with one of its declared errors (IndexNotFound,
InvalidArgumentEncoding, ...) by raising {CORE DECLARED-ERROR NAME}; the
call that invoked it turns that into an ordinary error completion, which
the program's handlers see. The code is printed only when the signal
escaped that conversion:
  * the Tcl compiler (compile) calls a native's implementation directly,
    without the conversion, where the compiler proved the call's bounds
    checks cannot fail; if that proof were wrong, the error would escape
    as CORE DECLARED-ERROR NAME;
  * a native that declares no errors raised one.
Either is a bug in the compiler or the runtime, not in the program.

== WHAT TO DO
Report the program and the backend. Running it on interp shows the
error the program was meant to see (an ordinary, handleable error, or
UNCAUGHT-ERROR at the top).

@topic error.core-malformed
title: CORE MALFORMED: core IR text that is not well-formed
summary: CORE MALFORMED rejects core IR (.ir files) whose nodes are not well-formed -- an unknown operation, a missing part, a bad literal; it is never a Botlish source error.
index: errors
alias: malformed IR; malformed core IR
keywords: .ir, core IR, node, operation, literal, interp, compile
related: backend.interp backend.compile error.native-aot-input error.hir-parse error.struct
docs: README.md DIRECT-HIR-NATIVE-PATH.md
code: CORE MALFORMED

== MEANING
Core IR is the small executable representation of the Tcl reference
interpreter: a list of nodes such as {call {ref +} {const 1} {const 2}}.
.ir files (examples/*.ir) are core IR text, run on interp and compile
only. A node that is not well-formed is rejected, never guessed at:

    malformed IR: unknown operation "frobnicate": frobnicate {const 1}
    malformed IR: expected USAGE: NODE
    malformed IR: not a canonical integer literal: NODE

Text that is not even a list of nodes (an unbalanced brace) fails earlier,
with "core::readProgramFile: FILE is not a list of IR expressions".

A .bot program lowers to core IR only on interp (hir::lower); a malformed
node there would be a compiler bug.

== WHAT TO DO
Fix the node the message quotes; README.md section 4 lists every form.
Prefer writing Botlish source: .bot files are checked, and they run on
every backend.

@topic error.core-process-argv
title: CORE PROCESS ARGV: an invalid injected argument vector
summary: CORE PROCESS ARGV rejects an argument vector given to a run (the compiler's -argv-hex) that contains a NUL byte, which no real process argument can.
index: errors
alias: injected argv; argv NUL byte
keywords: argv, -argv, -argv-hex, NUL, bytes, arguments, process, snapshot
related: concept.argv stdlib.argv cli.options task.read-arguments
docs: ARGV.md
code: CORE PROCESS ARGV

== MEANING
Before a run starts, not an error of the program. the compiler's -argv TEXT,
-argv-hex HEX and -argv-none give the program the argument vector argv()
returns. A Linux argument is a byte string without NUL, so a vector with
a NUL byte (-argv-hex 00) is refused before anything runs:

    error: argv element 0 is not a byte string without NUL (every
    character must be 1..255) (CORE PROCESS ARGV)

Tools that call core::process::withArgv get it for a character outside
1..255 too.

== WHAT TO DO
Drop the 00 bytes. To test how a program handles arguments that are not
valid UTF-8, pass such bytes (-argv-hex ff): argv() then fails with the
declared error InvalidArgumentEncoding, which the program handles.

@topic error.hir-interchange-compiler
title: HIR INTERCHANGE COMPILER: a .hirx file built by another front end
summary: HIR INTERCHANGE COMPILER refuses a .hirx file written by a different front end (another revision of core/, hir/, surface/ or lib's Tcl, or another Tcl patch level); rebuild it from the source.
index: errors backends
alias: built by another front end; stale hirx file
keywords: hirx, interchange, fingerprint, front end, stale, revision, rebuild
related: backend.hir-interchange error.hir-interchange-format error.hir-interchange-input cli.options
docs: HIR-INTERCHANGE.md COMPILATION-ISOLATION.md
code: HIR INTERCHANGE COMPILER

== MEANING
When a .hirx file is read; not an error of the program. A .hirx file
(-emit-hir-interchange) holds an analyzed HIR in the front end's own
representation, together with the fingerprint of the front end that built
it: a checksum of every Tcl source file that can shape a HIR (core/,
hir/, surface/, lib's Tcl libraries) and the Tcl patch level. A HIR read
by another front end could be silently wrong, so the reader refuses it,
and there is no option to skip the check:

    error: HIR interchange text built by another front end (FINGERPRINT;
    this one is FINGERPRINT) (HIR INTERCHANGE COMPILER)

Changing the backends (compiler/, native/) keeps .hirx files valid. The
front-end cache (BOTLISH_HIR_CACHE) uses the same format but treats a
foreign entry as a miss, never as this error.

== WHAT TO DO
A .hirx file is a compilation result, not a source: regenerate it from the
.bot (or .hir) file with this compiler, -emit-hir-interchange FILE.bot.

@topic error.hir-interchange-format
title: HIR INTERCHANGE FORMAT: a file that is not HIR interchange text
summary: HIR INTERCHANGE FORMAT rejects a .hirx file whose header is not the interchange format's, whose version this compiler does not read, or which lacks its compiler or its HIR.
index: errors backends
alias: not HIR interchange text; hirx version
keywords: hirx, interchange, header, version, corrupt, truncated
related: backend.hir-interchange error.hir-interchange-compiler error.hir-interchange-input error.hir-parse
docs: HIR-INTERCHANGE.md
code: HIR INTERCHANGE FORMAT

== MEANING
When a .hirx file is read; not an error of the program. The file must
start with the line `botlish-hir-interchange 1` followed by a Tcl dict
holding the compiler's fingerprint and the HIR. The messages:

    not HIR interchange text (it starts "...")
    HIR interchange version V: this compiler reads version 1
    HIR interchange text without its compiler or its HIR

Usual causes: a file renamed to .hirx (a .hir text file is a different
format: HIR text), a truncated or edited file, or one written by a
compiler with a newer format version.

== WHAT TO DO
Regenerate the file with -emit-hir-interchange FILE.bot. Run a .hir file
under its own extension.

@topic error.hir-interchange-input
title: HIR INTERCHANGE INPUT: -emit-hir-interchange given the wrong file
summary: HIR INTERCHANGE INPUT rejects an input of -emit-hir-interchange that is not a .bot or .hir file: interchange text is written from HIR, never from core IR.
index: errors backends
alias: emit-hir-interchange input
keywords: -emit-hir-interchange, .ir, .hirx, input file, extension
related: backend.hir-interchange error.hir-interchange-format error.native-aot-input cli.options
docs: HIR-INTERCHANGE.md
code: HIR INTERCHANGE INPUT

== MEANING
On the command line, before anything is compiled. -emit-hir-interchange
writes FILE.hirx from the analyzed HIR of a .bot or .hir input; any other
input is refused, for example a core IR file:

    prog.ir: expected a .bot or .hir input file: prog.ir
    (HIR INTERCHANGE INPUT)

== WHAT TO DO
Give it the Botlish source (.bot) or HIR text (.hir).

@topic error.hir-interchange-target
title: HIR INTERCHANGE TARGET: a .hirx file built for another target
summary: HIR INTERCHANGE TARGET refuses a .hirx file whose HIR was built for another target (another operating system or architecture) than the compilation's; compile the program for the target you want.
index: errors backends
alias: hirx for another target
keywords: hirx, interchange, target, -target, operating system, architecture, C ABI
related: backend.hir-interchange backend.targets error.hir-interchange-compiler error.native-target cli.options
docs: HIR-INTERCHANGE.md MACOS-AARCH64-MILESTONE-2.md
code: HIR INTERCHANGE TARGET

== MEANING
When a .hirx file is read; not an error of the program. A HIR whose
build depended on the target (it imports ffi::c, an
architecture-specific module or a platform namespace) records that
target, and is valid for it alone: its C integer types have that
target's sizes, and its modules are that target's. Reading it for
another target (main.tcl's -target, or the host's default) is refused,
never reinterpreted:

    HIR interchange text for another target: it was built for target
    linux-aarch64, not macos-aarch64: a HIR keeps the target it was built
    for (its C ABI facts and platform modules) and is never
    reinterpreted as another's; compile the program for macos-aarch64

A HIR that depended on no target is every target's and reads anywhere.
The front-end cache keeps one entry per target and never reports this.

== WHAT TO DO
Run the file with the target it was built for (`-target NAME`), or
regenerate it for this one: `-target NAME -emit-hir-interchange
FILE.bot`.

@topic error.hir-parse
title: HIR PARSE: HIR text that is malformed or inconsistent
summary: HIR PARSE rejects a .hir file (hir::format's notation used as input) that is malformed or internally inconsistent: a bad header, an id declared twice, a reference to an invisible binding.
index: errors
alias: malformed HIR text; .hir file error
keywords: .hir, HIR text, hir::parse, hir::readFile, header, binding id, scope
related: error.hir-interchange-format error.core-malformed backend.hir-interchange cli.usage
docs: README.md
code: HIR PARSE

== MEANING
When a .hir file is read; not an error of a Botlish program. HIR text is
the semantic HIR as hir::format (-hir) prints it, readable back as a
program (examples/hir/*.hir). The reader checks that the text is
well-formed and consistent -- a header line `program SCOPE ...`, every id
declared once, every reference to a visible binding, a block's type
naming that block, no forward reference -- and reports the first problem:

    HIR text line N: expected a header "program SCOPE ?binds ...?" or
    "ambient SCOPE"
    HIR text: expression E refers to "x" (B), which is not established
    until E2: forward references are not allowed

It does not check that the stated facts (types, targets) are true.

Run directly (botlish FILE.hir) the error is shown with a Tcl stack trace
whose first line is the message; -emit-hir-interchange and
-emit-native-executable print it as "FILE: MESSAGE (HIR PARSE)".

== WHAT TO DO
Fix the line the message names, or regenerate the text from the source
(botlish -hir FILE.bot). HIR text is for compiler development; programs
are written in Botlish source.

@topic error.hir-range-limit
title: HIR RANGE LIMIT: the native range analysis did not converge
summary: HIR RANGE LIMIT is a compiler limit: native lowering's interprocedural integer-range fixpoint did not settle within its round budget; the program is not at fault.
index: errors backends
alias: closed-call facts did not converge
keywords: range analysis, fixpoint, convergence, budget, native lowering, cranelift
related: error.hir-specialize-limit concept.specialization backend.cranelift backend.interp proof.ranges error.native-bug
docs: README.md CRANELIFT-WASM.md
code: HIR RANGE LIMIT

== MEANING
At compile time, on the native backends only (cranelift,
cranelift-generic, -emit-native-executable, -emit-nir, -emit-clif); a
limit of the compiler, not an error of the program. Before emitting
native code, the compiler computes integer ranges across function
instances (to choose small-integer representations and drop checks),
repeating rounds until nothing changes. Its budget is 4 rounds per
instance plus 16; past it, compilation stops with "hir::range:
closed-call facts did not converge" rather than guess. The analysis is
designed to converge, so reaching the budget points at a bug in it.

== WHAT TO DO
Run the program on interp or compile, which do not run this analysis.
Report the program (the smallest version that still fails helps most).

@topic error.hir-specialize-limit
title: HIR SPECIALIZE LIMIT: call-site specialization did not converge
summary: HIR SPECIALIZE LIMIT is a compiler limit: the native backend's call-site specialization fixpoint did not settle within its analysis budget; the program is not at fault.
index: errors backends
alias: specialization did not converge
keywords: specialization, instances, fixpoint, convergence, budget, cranelift, aot-spec
related: concept.specialization error.hir-range-limit backend.cranelift backend.cranelift-generic backend.native-executable error.native-bug
docs: README.md
code: HIR SPECIALIZE LIMIT

== MEANING
At compile time, where call-site specialization runs: -backend cranelift,
-emit-native-executable, -emit-nir and -emit-clif, and -aot-spec. A limit
of the compiler, not an error of the program. Specialization creates
function instances keyed by the argument kinds their call sites prove
(concept.specialization) and re-analyzes them until their facts settle;
past 64 analyses per allowed instance (instances are capped at 1000), it
stops with "hir::specialize: the fixpoint did not converge".

== WHAT TO DO
Run the program with -backend cranelift-generic, which does not
specialize, or on interp or compile. Report the program.

@topic error.native-address
title: NATIVE ADDRESS: a C function returned an address Botlish cannot hold
summary: NATIVE ADDRESS stops a run when a C function's pointer result does not fit Botlish's C pointer word (a sign-extended 60-bit address): the pointer is never truncated or masked.
index: errors backends
alias: C pointer too wide; tagged pointer result
keywords: extern c fn, pointer, address, ffi::c::Ptr, top byte, tagged pointer, aarch64
related: concept.c-ffi backend.native-only error.native-bool error.native-contract-violation
docs: C-FFI.md C-ABI-TYPES.md
code: NATIVE ADDRESS

== MEANING
Run time, native backends only (a C call runs nowhere else). An
ffi::c::Ptr is a tagged word holding a sign-extended 60-bit address. Every
canonical x86-64 address and every untagged AArch64 address fits; a
top-byte-tagged AArch64 pointer, or a garbage value returned as a pointer,
does not, and the call fails with "the C function NAME returned the
address 0x..., which a Botlish C pointer cannot hold ...".

== WHAT TO DO
Check the declaration against the C header: a function declared to return
a pointer that really returns an integer or a tagged pointer is
mis-declared. A C library that tags its pointers needs a C wrapper that
returns them untagged.

@topic error.native-aot-not-ready
title: NATIVE AOT NOT-READY: the program cannot be compiled ahead of time
summary: NATIVE AOT NOT-READY refuses -emit-native-executable when some function instance the program uses is not closed: an operand's kind or a call's target is not known statically.
index: errors backends
alias: not AOT-ready; program is not AOT-ready
keywords: -emit-native-executable, -aot-spec, closed, guarded, open, annotation, parameter type, blocker
related: backend.native-executable concept.specialization backend.cranelift error.native-link
docs: README.md SINGLE-EXECUTABLE.md
code: NATIVE AOT NOT-READY

== MEANING
At compile time, for -emit-native-executable only; the program is valid
and runs on every backend, but not in the form a standalone executable
needs. An executable contains no fallback: every function instance it
uses must be *closed*, every operation chosen and every operand's kind
known. An instance is *guarded* when some operand's kind is unknown (an
untyped parameter no call site determines) and *open* when an operation
cannot be chosen (a call of a value of unknown kind). The error lists each
such instance and its blockers; nothing is written.

== EXAMPLE
    fn make_adder(x):
        fn add(y):
            x + y

        add

    add10 = make_adder(10)
    add10(32)

runs everywhere (42), but -emit-native-executable adder.bot reports

    adder.bot: program is not AOT-ready:
    add<generic>: guarded
      line 3:13: + needs int; parameter y (no annotation, no call-site
      inference) has no static kind (NATIVE AOT NOT-READY)

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
botlish -aot-spec FILE.bot prints every function instance with its
status, parameter types and blockers, then runs the program.

== WHAT TO DO
Annotate what the analysis cannot infer: here `fn make_adder(x: int):` and
`fn add(y: int) -> int:` make every instance closed. Avoid `any` and
calls of values of unknown kind in code an executable runs. The JIT
backends (-backend cranelift) run guarded code as it is.

@topic error.native-aot-input
title: NATIVE AOT INPUT: core IR given to a native backend
summary: NATIVE AOT INPUT refuses a core IR (.ir) file given to a native backend, -emit-nir, -emit-clif or -emit-native-executable: native compilation starts from HIR, never from core IR.
index: errors backends
alias: core IR on a native backend; ir file with cranelift
keywords: .ir, core IR, cranelift, -emit-native-executable, -emit-nir, HIR, .hir, .hirx
related: error.core-malformed backend.cranelift backend.native-executable error.hir-interchange-input cli.options
docs: DIRECT-HIR-NATIVE-PATH.md
code: NATIVE AOT INPUT

== MEANING
On the command line, before anything is compiled. Native code is compiled
from the analyzed HIR (.bot source, .hir text or a .hirx file); core IR
is the interpreter's lower-level form and lacks what native lowering needs
(declared types, module identity, `errors` clauses). So a .ir file is
refused:

    error: prog.ir is core IR text; native compilation starts from HIR
    (.bot or .hir), never core IR (NATIVE AOT INPUT)

and with -emit-native-executable, "expected a .bot, .hir or .hirx input
file (native executables are built from HIR, never from core IR text)".

== WHAT TO DO
Run a .ir file on interp or compile. For native code, use the program's
.bot source, or a .hir file (examples/hir/ has .hir versions of the .ir
samples).

@topic error.native-bool
title: NATIVE BOOL: a C function returned a _Bool that is neither 0 nor 1
summary: NATIVE BOOL stops a run when a C function declared to return bool returns a result byte other than 0 or 1: the declaration does not describe the function.
index: errors backends
alias: _Bool result neither 0 nor 1
keywords: extern c fn, bool, _Bool, result, ffi, declaration
related: concept.c-ffi error.native-address error.native-contract-violation
docs: C-FFI.md
code: NATIVE BOOL

== MEANING
Run time, native backends only. A C _Bool result is the byte 0 or 1; no C
compiler returns anything else. A declaration `-> bool` of a function that
returns another byte (it really returns an int, a char, a pointer) gets
"the C function NAME returned the byte 0x.. as its _Bool result, which is
neither 0 nor 1: no C compiler returns such a _Bool, so its extern c fn
declaration does not describe it". The byte is never read as one bit.

== WHAT TO DO
Correct the `extern c fn` declaration's result type to the C function's
real one (an ffi::c integer type), and compare it with 0 in Botlish.

@topic error.native-bug
title: NATIVE BUG: an internal failure of the native backend
summary: NATIVE BUG reports an internal failure of the native backend -- native lowering, the botlish-native driver, an executable -- or a driver that died without a result.
index: errors backends
alias: native backend panicked; native backend failed
keywords: panic, crash, internal error, lowering, driver, invariant, report, cranelift
related: error.native-codegen error.native-invalid-nir error.native-invalid-hir backend.cranelift backend.cranelift-generic backend.interp
docs: README.md DIRECT-HIR-NATIVE-PATH.md
code: NATIVE BUG

== MEANING
At compile or run time, on the native backends; a bug in the compiler or
the native runtime, not in the program. Its forms:
  * "native lowering: ..." -- native/lower.tcl found HIR facts that
    contradict each other while emitting NIR;
  * "the native backend panicked: ..." -- the Rust driver hit an
    internal error;
  * "native backend failed: ..." or "native backend produced no
    result" -- the driver process ended without reporting (a crash, or
    a process killed from outside, e.g. for lack of memory);
  * "native executable panicked" -- a standalone executable's runtime.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
Run the program on -backend cranelift-generic and on interp: if they
succeed, the bug is in specialized lowering. -emit-nir shows the NIR the
program lowers to. A smaller program that still fails makes a good report.

== WHAT TO DO
Report the program, the backend and the message. Meanwhile run it on
another backend.

@topic error.native-codegen
title: NATIVE CODEGEN: Cranelift rejected the generated code
summary: NATIVE CODEGEN reports that Cranelift's verifier or code generator rejected the code the native backend produced, or could not target this host; a compiler bug or an unsupported machine.
index: errors backends
alias: Cranelift verifier error; Cranelift rejected the code
keywords: cranelift, verifier, codegen, CLIF, ISA, host, machine code
related: error.native-bug error.native-invalid-nir backend.cranelift backend.platforms
docs: README.md CRANELIFT-WASM.md
code: NATIVE CODEGEN

== MEANING
At compile time, on the native backends; a bug in the native backend, or
a host Cranelift cannot generate code for. The driver translates NIR to
Cranelift IR (shown by -emit-clif); when Cranelift's verifier or its code
generation fails, or its ISA builder rejects the host, the message is
Cranelift's own, usually naming the function. One refusal is the native
backend's own: "variadic C call of NAME: no variadic call lowering for the
windows_fastcall convention" -- a variadic C call (concept.variadics) on
Windows x86-64, which has none yet.

== WHAT TO DO
Check that the host is a supported platform (backend.platforms). Otherwise
report the program with the -emit-clif output, and run it on interp or
compile meanwhile.

@topic error.native-contract-violation
title: NATIVE CONTRACT-VIOLATION: a C function broke its declared contract
summary: NATIVE CONTRACT-VIOLATION stops a run when a C function declared to return its pointer argument (alias P) returns a different address: the binding's contract is false.
index: errors backends
alias: alias contract is false; extern alias result mismatch
keywords: extern c fn, alias, contract, pointer result, provenance, ffi
related: concept.c-ffi error.native-address error.native-bool
docs: C-FFI.md C-POINTER-BORROWING.md
code: NATIVE CONTRACT-VIOLATION

== MEANING
Run time, native backends only; an error of the `extern c fn`
declaration, not of the calling code. A result fact `alias P` says the C
function returns exactly its pointer argument P. It is the one fact a run
can observe, so every call checks it right after returning: "the C
function NAME returned 0x..., but its extern c fn declaration says its
result is exactly its pointer argument (alias), 0x...: the binding's
contract is false". Other facts (noescape, read, write) describe C's code
and are never checked; a false one is undefined behaviour.

== WHAT TO DO
Read the C function's documentation: if it may return another pointer
(into the buffer, a new allocation), declare the result `independent` or
leave its provenance unknown (C-FFI.md, "Pointer results").

@topic error.native-invalid-hir
title: NATIVE INVALID-HIR: native lowering met HIR it cannot interpret
summary: NATIVE INVALID-HIR means native lowering met HIR no checked build produces (an unknown node kind, an undeclared enum or C function): hand-written HIR or a front-end bug.
index: errors backends
alias: native lowering invalid HIR
keywords: HIR, native lowering, .hir, enum, extern c fn, constant, front end
related: error.hir-parse error.native-bug error.native-invalid-nir backend.cranelift
docs: DIRECT-HIR-NATIVE-PATH.md README.md
code: NATIVE INVALID-HIR

== MEANING
At compile time, on the native backends. native/lower.tcl lowers the
analyzed HIR the front end built; its messages start "native lowering:"
and name what it could not interpret: an unknown HIR expression kind, an
enum or `extern c fn` the program does not declare, an enum case its
enum lacks, an unexpected constant or root value. A HIR built from .bot
source never has these, so the cause is HIR text (.hir) written or edited
by hand, or a bug in the front end.

== WHAT TO DO
For a .hir file, regenerate it from its source (botlish -hir FILE.bot).
For a .bot program, report it.

@topic error.native-invalid-nir
title: NATIVE INVALID-NIR: the native driver rejected the NIR
summary: NATIVE INVALID-NIR means the botlish-native driver's validator rejected the native IR it was given: usually a driver built from older sources than native/lower.tcl, otherwise a lowering bug.
index: errors backends
alias: invalid NIR; stale native build
keywords: NIR, botlish-native, validator, rebuild, cargo build, version
related: error.native-not-built error.native-bug error.native-codegen backend.cranelift
docs: README.md DIRECT-HIR-NATIVE-PATH.md
code: NATIVE INVALID-NIR

== MEANING
At compile time, on the native backends; not an error of the program.
native/lower.tcl writes NIR text, and the Rust driver parses and validates
it before generating code ("invalid NIR at line N: ..."). The two are built
from the same checkout: when native/ was updated (a pull, a branch switch)
and the driver was not rebuilt, the old driver may not know the new NIR.
Otherwise the lowering emitted NIR its own driver rejects: a bug.

== WHAT TO DO
Rebuild the native backend: cargo build --release --manifest-path
native/Cargo.toml. If the error persists, report the program with its
-emit-nir output.

@topic error.native-io
title: NATIVE IO: the native driver or an executable could not read or write
summary: NATIVE IO reports an input or output failure of the native machinery itself -- the driver reading its NIR or writing its result, an executable writing its value -- not of the program's own I/O.
index: errors backends
alias: executable cannot write its value
keywords: io, file, stdout, disk full, temporary directory, result file, executable
related: error.native-startup error.native-link backend.native-executable cli.output
docs: C-FFI.md SINGLE-EXECUTABLE.md
code: NATIVE IO

== MEANING
On the native backends and in executables; a problem of the
environment, not an error of the program. The forms:
  * the driver cannot read its NIR file or create its result file, or a
    program that calls C cannot create its private work directory or
    start its linked executable;
  * a standalone executable cannot write its final value, e.g. to a full
    disk or a closed standard output:

    No space left on device (os error 28) (NATIVE IO)

The program's own printing (io::print_line) reports failures as the
declared error io::WriteFailed instead.

== WHAT TO DO
Check the temporary directory (TMPDIR), free disk space and where the
standard output goes.

@topic error.native-limit-stack
title: NATIVE LIMIT STACK: recursion too deep for the native stack
summary: NATIVE LIMIT STACK stops a native run whose nested calls exhaust a native stack (a thread's or a coroutine's), and TCL LIMIT STACK a Tcl backend's: resource limits, not semantics.
index: errors backends
alias: stack overflow; recursion too deep; deep recursion; native stack exhausted
keywords: recursion, stack, depth, BOTLISH_NATIVE_STACK_BYTES, BOTLISH_NATIVE_COROUTINE_STACK_BYTES, coroutine, too many nested evaluations, limit, tail call
related: backend.cranelift backend.interp backend.compile backend.performance concept.loops
docs: NATIVE-STACK-OVERFLOW.md MACOS-AARCH64-MILESTONE-4.md WINDOWS-X86-64-MILESTONE-1.md COROUTINES.md README.md
code: NATIVE LIMIT STACK
code: TCL LIMIT STACK

== MEANING
Run time. Recursion depth is a resource limit, not semantics, so the
backends stop at different depths:
  * native backends and executables: native code runs on a 1 GiB stack,
    and a coroutine's body on an 8 MiB stack of its own; overflowing either
    stops the run with "native stack exhausted: too many nested calls"
    (NATIVE LIMIT STACK), never a crash, on Linux x86-64 and AArch64 and
    macOS AArch64. A standalone executable prints it on stderr as `error
    {NATIVE LIMIT STACK} {native stack exhausted: ...}` and exits 1. On
    Windows x86-64 there is no stack guard yet: Botlish recursion is
    stopped with the same error by the shadow stack's depth check (about
    four million nested calls), which the default 1 GiB stack outlasts;
    a smaller stack (BOTLISH_NATIVE_STACK_BYTES), or deep recursion inside
    the runtime, ends the process instead.
  * interp and compile: Tcl's evaluation limit, raised by the compiler to
    about 14,000 nested Botlish calls on interp and 99,000 on compile,
    stops the run with "too many nested evaluations (infinite loop?)"
    (TCL LIMIT STACK); on interp it takes about a minute to arrive.
Neither is a declared error: no handler catches it.

== EXAMPLE
    fn depth(n: int) -> int:
        if n == 0:
            return 0
        1 + depth(n - 1)

    depth(20000000)

stops on cranelift with "native stack exhausted: too many nested calls
(NATIVE LIMIT STACK)", and depth(200000) on compile with "too many nested
evaluations (infinite loop?) (TCL LIMIT STACK)".

== WHAT TO DO
Make sure the recursion ends (a missing or unreachable base case is the
usual cause). For a deep but finite computation, iterate instead
(concept.loops), or recurse on halves rather than on n - 1. On the native
backends and in executables, BOTLISH_NATIVE_STACK_BYTES=N (at least 1 MiB)
sets the stack size: the example above runs with 4294967296;
BOTLISH_NATIVE_COROUTINE_STACK_BYTES=N (at least 64 KiB) sets a coroutine
stack's.

@topic error.native-link
title: NATIVE LINK and NATIVE AOT: linking a native executable failed
summary: NATIVE LINK (a program that calls C) and NATIVE AOT (any other) report that linking a native executable failed: a missing C library or symbol, no rustc, a refused linker.
index: errors backends
alias: link failed; undefined symbol; cannot find library; executable link failed
keywords: linker, rustc, cc, ld, -l, -L, extern c library, BOTLISH_NATIVE_LINKER, stub, libbotlish_native
related: concept.c-ffi backend.native-executable backend.single-executable error.native-aot-not-ready error.native-not-built
docs: C-FFI.md SINGLE-EXECUTABLE.md
code: NATIVE LINK
code: NATIVE AOT

== MEANING
At link time: -emit-native-executable, and the native backends' runs of a
program that calls C (which are linked as executables, then run). Not an
error of the program's code. The code is NATIVE LINK when the program
declares C functions or libraries, NATIVE AOT otherwise.

NATIVE LINK:
  * "native link failed: the system linker could not link this program
    with the C functions and libraries it declares (symbols ...;
    libraries -l...):" followed by the linker's own lines -- a library
    the linker cannot find, or a symbol no library defines. A missing
    library is never a front-end error: it fails here, even in a
    program that calls nothing from it.
  * the stub linker (BOTLISH_NATIVE_LINKER=stub, the single executable's)
    refuses any program that calls C or declares a library.
  The linker's own lines are GNU ld's or lld's on Linux ("undefined
  reference to", "cannot find -lNAME", "unable to find library"), Apple
  ld's on macOS ("Undefined symbols for architecture arm64:" and a
  `"_SYMBOL", referenced from:` line per symbol, "ld: library 'NAME' not
  found", "found architecture 'x86_64', required architecture 'arm64'"),
  link.exe's on Windows ("error LNK2019: unresolved external symbol NAME",
  "fatal error LNK1181: cannot open input file 'NAME.lib'").
NATIVE AOT:
  * "executable link failed (STATUS): ..." with rustc's output, the system
    linker's own lines among it;
  * "cannot launch rustc to link executable", "runtime library missing:
    ..." (the native backend is not built), "unknown BOTLISH_NATIVE_LINKER",
    "the stub linker supports x86_64 Linux only", "executable emission
    requires Linux glibc on x86_64 or aarch64, or macOS on aarch64" (any
    other host).

== WHAT TO DO
For C: install the library (for the host's architecture), pass its
directory with -L DIR, and check each `named` symbol against the library:
a symbol is its C name, never Mach-O's `_`-prefixed spelling, which the
toolchain adds. On Windows `extern c library "NAME"` is the file NAME.lib
-- a static library, or a DLL's import library (the DLL itself must then
be beside the executable or on PATH when it runs) -- and every executable
links the static release C runtime (/MT, cl's default), so build a static
C library with /MT: a /MD one calls the C runtime through `__imp_` imports
the static runtime does not define, and link.exe reports them unresolved
("error LNK2019: unresolved external symbol __imp_NAME"; the message then
says so). A DLL may use either runtime (WINDOWS-X86-64-MILESTONE-2.md, "The
C runtime"). The Tcl backends never call C. For NATIVE AOT:
the default (rustc) linker needs rustc and a built native backend in the
checkout, and on macOS the Xcode command line tools (the system linker);
the stub linker needs neither but is Linux x86-64 only and links no C.

