related: backend.cranelift backend.single-executable error.native-invalid-nir error.native-link backend.interp
docs: README.md SINGLE-EXECUTABLE.md
code: NATIVE NOT-BUILT

== MEANING
Before native compilation; not an error of the program. The native
backends (cranelift, cranelift-generic, -emit-clif, executables) run the
Rust driver native/target/release/botlish-native. In a source checkout it
is built separately; until then every native run stops with

    error: native backend not built: run "cargo build --release" in
    .../native (NATIVE NOT-BUILT)

BOTLISH_NATIVE_BIN, when set, names the driver to use instead. The single
executable packs its drivers and installs them itself.

== WHAT TO DO
Build it: cargo build --release --manifest-path native/Cargo.toml. The
pinned Cranelift crates need a recent stable rustc; if cargo reports that
a package is not supported, update the toolchain (rustup toolchain install
stable; rustup default stable). Without a native build, interp and compile
run every program except the native-only operations.

@topic error.native-startup
title: NATIVE STARTUP: a standalone executable could not start
summary: NATIVE STARTUP means a stub-linked executable could not load the program image appended to it, or the runtime could not start its worker thread; the file is damaged or was built for another runtime.
index: errors backends
alias: no program image; bare botlish-aot-stub
keywords: executable, stub linker, program image, botlish-aot-stub, startup, thread, format
related: backend.native-executable backend.single-executable error.native-io error.native-link
docs: SINGLE-EXECUTABLE.md
code: NATIVE STARTUP

== MEANING
When a standalone executable starts, before the program runs; not an
error of the program. A stub-linked executable (BOTLISH_NATIVE_LINKER=stub,
what the single executable uses) is the prebuilt botlish-aot-stub with the
program's image appended; at startup it reads the image back from its own
file. The messages:

    /proc/self/exe: no program image (this is the bare botlish-aot-stub,
    not an executable it linked)
    FILE: truncated program image
    program image was compiled for a different native runtime build
    program image format N, this runtime reads format M

It is also reported when the runtime cannot create its worker thread
(for example a BOTLISH_NATIVE_STACK_BYTES the system refuses), and by
botlish-aot-stub on a machine other than x86-64 Linux.

== WHAT TO DO
Rebuild the executable with -emit-native-executable, and copy it whole
(never strip or edit it: the image is at its end).

@topic error.native-target
title: NATIVE TARGET: native code for another target's program
summary: NATIVE TARGET refuses to run or emit machine code for a program built for another target than the host's; run it on its own target's host or on a Tcl backend.
index: errors backends
alias: built for another target; native code for another target
keywords: target, -target, triple, host, macOS, Linux, aarch64, cross compile, native
related: backend.targets backend.platforms error.unsupported-platform error.hir-interchange-target backend.interp backend.native-executable
docs: MACOS-AARCH64-MILESTONE-2.md C-ABI-TYPES.md
code: NATIVE TARGET

== MEANING
Before native code runs or is emitted; not an error of the program. A
program whose build depended on the target (ffi::c, an
architecture-specific module, a platform namespace) carries that target
to the native backend. Native code is made for the host's target only:
the native backends refuse to run another target's program, and
-emit-native-executable to build it:

    this program was built for target linux-aarch64
    (aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu), and native code runs only on its own
    target's host: this host is aarch64-apple-darwin. Both are aarch64,
    but a matching architecture is not a matching target. Compile the
    program for this host (main.tcl -target, or the host's default), or
    run it on a Tcl backend

The Tcl backends (interp, compile) run a program built for any target:
they execute no target machine code, and refuse C calls and system calls
themselves.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * -target naming another operating system or architecture, with a
    native backend.
  * A Linux program compiled for linux-aarch64 on a Mac (the only way to
    compile one there, error.unsupported-platform), run natively.

== WHAT TO DO
Drop -target to compile for the host, run the program on interp or
compile, or run it on a host of its target.

@topic error.native-unsupported
title: NATIVE UNSUPPORTED: a construct the native backend does not support
summary: NATIVE UNSUPPORTED KIND reports a valid construct the native backends cannot handle: a field of unknown shape, a context too complex for a fixed slot, a function as the result.
index: errors backends
alias: native lowering does not support; not supported natively
keywords: struct-shape, CONTEXT-NATIVE-LOWERING-UNSUPPORTED, value, foreign, sequence-mode, validator contract, cranelift-generic, context struct
related: backend.cranelift backend.cranelift-generic backend.native-only concept.contexts concept.specialization error.native-bug
docs: README.md CONTEXTS.md DIRECT-HIR-NATIVE-PATH.md
code: NATIVE UNSUPPORTED
code: NATIVE UNSUPPORTED CONTEXT-NATIVE-LOWERING-UNSUPPORTED
code: NATIVE UNSUPPORTED struct-shape

== MEANING
On the native backends; the program is valid and runs on interp and
compile, but the native backend cannot handle part of it. The third word
of the code says what:
  * struct-shape: a field projection whose receiver's struct type is not
    known statically, in unspecialized code (cranelift-generic); native
    code reads a field from a known slot, never by name.
  * CONTEXT-NATIVE-LOWERING-UNSUPPORTED: an installed context struct with
    a field that cannot live in the fixed native context area -- a str,
    a List, a struct holding one, or an Int whose type does not bound it
    to the small-integer range (plain `int`). Bool, unit, UnicodeChar,
    enum cases, MutableVector, MutableArray and bounded integer domains
    are supported.
  * value: the program's final value is a function or a coroutine
    handle, which the driver cannot hand back to the compiler. The same code
    also reports the Linux syscall transport of another architecture and
    native coroutines on an unsupported platform.
  * native NAME: a native implemented only in Tcl (the regex library, the
    test suite's instrumentation natives).
  * foreign: batch timing or allocation reports of a program that calls
    C, or such a program run with an empty argument vector.
  * validator contract, ambient binding, DynamicBinding,
    UnresolvedControl, sequence-mode: HIR that only unchecked programs or
    the embedding API produce.
Lowering messages start with the source location and the HIR node.

== EXAMPLES
    struct A:
        x: int

    struct B:
        y: int
        x: int

    fn getx(p):
        p.x

    getx(A {x: 1}) + getx(B {y: 2, x: 3})

gives 4 on interp, compile and cranelift (which specializes getx for each
struct); on cranelift-generic it fails with "the field projection ".x"
has a receiver of type any, so its slot is not statically known (NATIVE
UNSUPPORTED struct-shape)".

    context struct Limits:
        depth: int

installed with `with context Limits {depth: 3}` fails on the native
backends: "field "depth" is an Int (int) whose declared domain does not
fit the small-Int representation ... (NATIVE UNSUPPORTED
CONTEXT-NATIVE-LOWERING-UNSUPPORTED)". Declaring `type Depth = Int in
0..1000` and `depth: Depth` makes it compile.

== WHAT TO DO
  * struct-shape: run on cranelift, or give the parameter its struct
    type.
  * contexts: bound Int fields with an integer domain; pass strings and
    Lists as ordinary parameters (or keep them in a MutableVector field).
  * value: end the program with data (call the function), not with a
    function.
  * anything else: run on interp or compile, and report the program if
    it is ordinary Botlish source.

@topic error.surface-syntax
title: SURFACE SYNTAX: the source does not parse
summary: SURFACE SYNTAX reports Botlish source that does not parse; every malformed construct is reported once, with its line and column, and a file with syntax errors is not checked further.
index: errors rules
alias: syntax error; parse error; does not parse; unexpected token
keywords: parser, lexer, expected, found, unterminated string, never closed, unexpected character, indentation, colon, recovery
related: concept.syntax rule.if-placement rule.loop-placement rule.one-handler-per-error rule.comparison-chains rule.indentation rule.names rule.no-list-destructuring error.list-destructuring
docs: DIAGNOSTIC-RECOVERY.md README.md
code: SURFACE SYNTAX

== MEANING
At compile time, before any other check. The lexer and the parser read a
.bot file into a syntax tree; text that does not fit the grammar is a
syntax error, reported as FILE:LINE:COLUMN: MESSAGE (SURFACE SYNTAX).
A file with syntax errors is not analyzed further: its semantic
diagnostics (TYPE, UNBOUND, ...) appear once it parses.

Some syntax errors carry a code of their own at the start of the message
(LIST-DESTRUCTURING: ..., EXTERN-SYNTAX: ...); each has its own page.

== HOW ERRORS ARE REPORTED
Every independent error is reported, in source order, in one compilation:
  * a statement that fails to parse is one error; parsing resumes after
    its line and the block indented under it, and an `elif` or `else`
    right after it belongs to it (not reported again);
  * the lexer recovers locally -- it skips an unexpected character, ends
    an unterminated string at the end of the line, closes a bracket left
    open at the end of input or before a line that starts like a new
    statement -- and errors caused by its own guesses inside a malformed
    construct are not reported;
  * two malformed constructs are two errors, and two brackets left open
    are two.
Recovery is by grammar boundaries, never by comparing messages
(DIAGNOSTIC-RECOVERY.md, section 5).

== THE MAIN FAMILIES
  expected X, found Y        the commonest form, from the parser: Y is
                             "end of line", "indentation", "end of block",
                             "end of file", name "x", integer 3, or a
                             token: expected ":" after the "if"
                             condition, found end of line
  "(" is never closed        a bracket left open (also "]" or "}"); "}"
                             does not match "(" opened at L:C
  unterminated string        a string with no closing quote on its line
                             (also: unterminated character literal,
                             invalid escape, empty character literal)
  unexpected character "$"   a character outside the language
  indentation                tab used for indentation; inconsistent
                             dedent; unexpected indentation
  names and literals         an identifier with more than one trailing
                             "?"; leading zeros in an integer
  placement                  an "if" value must be the whole right side
                             of "=" or "return"; "else" without a
                             matching "if"; a declaration nested in a
                             function; an import after other statements
  absent forms               comparison chaining, handler guards, several
                             errors in one handler, import aliases and
                             wildcards, positional destructuring

Most messages for absent forms say what to write instead. The rules
behind the commonest errors have their own pages (listed below), and the
help line printed under a syntax error names its rule's page when there is
one.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
Read the first error first: the column points at the token where parsing
went wrong, which may be after the real mistake (a missing ":" or ")" on
the previous line). Fix one error, compile again, and repeat.

@example reject
fn add(a: int, b: int) -> int:
    a + b

total = add(1, 2
result = total * 2
result
@caption An unclosed parenthesis: the next statement line closes it
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 4:12
@message "(" is never closed
@end
